# Who may sign a Maelys release.
#
# The ssh allowed_signers format, read by `ssh-keygen -Y verify`. The release
# workflow checks the tag's own signature against this file, at the socle
# commit the product pinned, and refuses a tag signed by anything else --
# GitHub saying `verified` only means the key belongs to some account, and
# every account that may push a tag has such a key. `preflight` reads it too,
# before there is a tag: it names the key this checkout would sign with.
#
# Fail closed. A missing file, an empty one, a key that is not here: the
# release stops. Nothing falls back to "verified by GitHub" alone.
#
# **A key is retired, never deleted.** Old tags keep their signatures, and a
# publication is replayed on the tag it already has, so a line removed here
# turns a past release into one that can no longer be republished. Give it
# `valid-before` instead, with the day it stopped signing, and leave it:
#
#   someone@example.org namespaces="git",valid-before="20260901" ssh-ed25519 AAAA...
#
# The time is YYYYMMDD, or YYYYMMDDHHMM[SS], with an optional trailing Z and
# no separator: ssh-keygen reads nothing else, and without the Z it reads
# the local time of whoever runs it. Write the Z.
#
# `valid-after` does the same at the other end for a key that starts today.
#
# An ssh signature carries no timestamp, so ssh-keygen judges both against
# the clock of whoever runs it -- which would make a retirement break every
# tag that key ever signed, the day it happens. The workflows give it the
# moment GitHub saw the tag, `verification.verified_at`, and stop when that
# is missing. Not the tagger date: that one sits in the payload the signer
# writes, and a stolen key backdates its tag and walks through its own
# retirement. Measured, both ways.
#
# **These options rotate a key; they do not revoke one.** A key that is
# still on its owner's GitHub account can be used to push a tag, and a
# retirement written here reaches a product only when that product adopts a
# socle carrying it. The lever that reaches every pin the same evening is
# removing the key from the GitHub account: GitHub then refuses to verify
# any new tag it signs, and every release workflow of the fleet already
# requires that verdict. SECURITY.md says it in order.
#
# `preflight` and `cut` judge against today, because the tag they are about
# to sign is signed today.
#
# One namespace: git. A key that signs tags signs nothing else here.
davidb987654@gmail.com namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIPjvkRbQYfr09ZNh5AzHIILBRw8XbiOFHrmEczoq6KXH
